Subject: [REVIEW 12/16] Thunderbolt: delay USB3 re-activation when resume discovery tears down an enabled adapter

tb_resume_noirq() waits 500 ms before re-activating USB3 tunnels only when discovery returns a USB3 tunnel. Discovery also disables an enabled USB3 downstream adapter when it cannot follow the whole path, for example when a dock router lost its configuration in sleep, and then returns no tunnel. Re-enabling that adapter before its USB3 link (ULV) has gone down, 40-80 ms on the tested hardware, lost every USB3 device behind the dock. Set the delay whenever an enabled USB3 downstream adapter exists before discovery, which includes the existing case. A bounded wait for ULV to clear could replace the fixed 500 ms. Independent of the rest of the series; the file is otherwise stock 7.3-rc2.

Dependencies: None for building. Patch 16 needs it at runtime for USB3 devices behind a dock to return after docked sleep.

Source attribution: Local change (candidate 1.151) to upstream code by Mika Westerberg, who added the delay in 43bddb26e20a; the adapter timing was measured with the diagnostic candidate 1.150, which is not included. Not posted.

This is a review grouping of preserved source, not a new authorship claim or
an independently build-validated upstream commit. Original attribution remains
in the referenced provenance files. No new Signed-off-by trailers are added.

---
diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c
index 47753a5c0f2ebde1dd4701b3b54e35561bdf0364..12b068a572d53a94345cd56c57f27e29052ab00e 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c
@@ -3159,6 +3159,26 @@ static void tb_free_unplugged_xdomains(struct tb_switch *sw)
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Is a USB3 downstream adapter enabled anywhere below @sw? Tunnel discovery
+ * disables such an adapter also when it cannot follow the whole path (for
+ * example when a device router lost its configuration in sleep), and it
+ * must not be re-enabled before its USB3 link has gone down.
+ */
+static bool tb_switch_usb3_enabled(struct tb_switch *sw)
+{
+	struct tb_port *port;
+
+	tb_switch_for_each_port(sw, port) {
+		if (tb_port_is_usb3_down(port) && tb_usb3_port_is_enabled(port))
+			return true;
+		if (tb_port_has_remote(port) &&
+		    tb_switch_usb3_enabled(port->remote->sw))
+			return true;
+	}
+	return false;
+}
+
 static int tb_resume_noirq(struct tb *tb)
 {
 	struct tb_cm *tcm = tb_priv(tb);
@@ -3181,6 +3201,10 @@ static int tb_resume_noirq(struct tb *tb)
 	tb_free_unplugged_xdomains(tb->root_switch);
 	tb_restore_children(tb->root_switch);
 
+	/* Discovery below tears down any enabled USB3 adapter */
+	if (tb_switch_usb3_enabled(tb->root_switch))
+		usb3_delay = 500;
+
 	/*
 	 * If we get here from suspend to disk the boot firmware or the
 	 * restore kernel might have created tunnels of its own. Since
@@ -3189,8 +3213,6 @@ static int tb_resume_noirq(struct tb *tb)
 	 */
 	tb_switch_discover_tunnels(tb->root_switch, &tunnels, false);
 	list_for_each_entry_safe_reverse(tunnel, n, &tunnels, list) {
-		if (tb_tunnel_is_usb3(tunnel))
-			usb3_delay = 500;
 		tb_tunnel_deactivate(tunnel);
 		tb_tunnel_put(tunnel);
 	}
